    "id": "fd_0133",
    "title": "Direction 4 (Grand Challenge): GL(2) Langlands via Automorphic Forms on Ad\u00e8les",
    "description": "**Conjecture:** The restricted product framework formalized here extends to GL(2): the group GL_2(\ud835\udd38_\u211a) can be formalized as a restricted product of GL_2(\u211a_p) with GL_2(\u2124_p) as integral subgroups, and automorphic forms on GL_2(\ud835\udd38_\u211a) (functions satisfying appropriate invariance, growth, and eigenvalue conditions) can be formalized using the `RestrictedProductData` structure with non-abelian local groups.\n\n**Test:** Generalize `RestrictedProductData` to allow non-commutative groups (replacing `CommGroup` with `Group`). Define a type `AutomorphicForm` on the restricted product as a function satisfying left-invariance by GL_2(\u211a) and right-invariance by a compact open subgroup. Verify that the space of automorphic forms is a module over the Hecke algebra.\n\nA disproof: if the non-commutativity of GL_2 causes the restricted product subgroup construction to fail (e.g., if the proof of `isRestrictedFamily_mul` requires commutativity in an essential way), we would need to restructure the framework.\n\n**Impact:** This would demonstrate that our GL(1) formalization is not a dead end but genuinely serves as a template for the full Langlands program.\n\n**Catalog References:** `LanglandsGL1/RestrictedProduct.lean` (RestrictedProductData, restrictedSubgroup, isRestrictedFamily_mul)\n\n**Proof Strategy:** The proof of `isRestrictedFamily_mul` uses `Subgroup.mul_mem_cancel_left/right`, which works for non-commutative groups. So the restricted product construction should generalize directly. The new challenge is defining automorphic forms as sections satisfying representation-theoretic conditions.\n\n**Domain Bridges:** Representation theory, harmonic analysis, modular forms\n\n**Lineage:** Generalizes `RestrictedProductData`; motivates by the connection to elliptic curves via modularity.\n\n**Ambition:** \u2605\u2605\u2605\u2605\u2605 \u2014 Would open GL(2) formalization in Lean 4.\n\n---",
